    {
        "id": 'eos.srk_peneloux_shift',
        "name": 'Soave-Redlich-Kwong Peneloux volume-translation parameter',
        "equation": 'c = 0.40768*(0.29441 - Z_RA)*R*Tc/Pc,  Z_RA = 0.29056 - 0.08775*omega',
        "source": {'standard': 'Péneloux, A.; Rauzy, E.; Fréze, R. (1982)', 'edition': 'A consistent correction for Redlich-Kwong-Soave volumes. Fluid Phase Equilibria, 8(1), 7-23', 'doi': '10.1016/0378-3812(82)80002-2'},
        "inputs": {'omega': {'unit': 'dimensionless', 'description': 'Acentric factor of the pure component, as in `eos.srk_kappa`.\n'}, 'Tc': {'unit': 'K', 'description': 'Critical temperature of the pure component.\n'}, 'Pc': {'unit': 'Pa', 'description': 'Critical pressure of the pure component.\n'}},
        "outputs": {'c': {'unit': 'm**3/mol', 'description': 'The volume-translation parameter. A property of the substance alone; `v_corr = v - c` where `v` is the untranslated molar volume.\n'}},
        "valid_range": [{'quantity': 'Tc', 'min': 0, 'min_inclusive': False, 'severity': 'error', 'rationale': '`Tc` is a factor in the numerator, so a non-positive value would give a non-positive or reversed shift, and a critical temperature at or below zero is not a substance.\n'}, {'quantity': 'Pc', 'min': 0, 'min_inclusive': False, 'severity': 'error', 'rationale': '`Pc` is a divisor; zero pressure is not a critical point and a negative one is not a state.\n'}],
        "assumptions": ['the acentric factor, critical temperature and critical pressure are those of the component intended. NOT CHECKED - this calc has no databank and no second source; all three come from the caller and are taken at face value.\n', '`Z_RA` is the Rackett compressibility from `Z_RA = 0.29056 - 0.08775*omega`. NeqSim defaults to it when a databank Rackett Z is absent, and this library ships no Rackett Z column, so the default is the whole correlation.\n', "`R` is the exact molar gas constant from the 2019 SI definition, not NeqSim's truncated `8.3144621` - the same value `eos.pr_molar_volume` uses, and the shift is subtracted from that calc's volume.\n", 'the shift is a volume translation only: it corrects the reported molar volume and density and does not change phase equilibrium.\n', "applying the shift is the caller's step: `v_corr = v - sum_i x_i*c_i`, with `v` from `eos.pr_molar_volume` and the per-component shifts mixed linearly.\n"],
        "references": ['Péneloux, A.; Rauzy, E.; Fréze, R. (1982). \\"A consistent correction for Redlich-Kwong-Soave volumes.\\" Fluid Phase Equilibria 8(1), 7-23. DOI 10.1016/0378-3812(82)80002-2.', 'Rackett, H. G. (1970). \\"Equation of state for saturated liquids.\\" J. Chem. Eng. Data 15(4), 514-517. DOI 10.1021/je60047a012.'],
        "solver": None,
        "data": None,
        "worked_example": {'source': 'derived from the equation above', 'derivation': "Propane-like, omega = 0.152, Tc = 369.83 K, Pc = 4.248e6 Pa:\n\n  Z_RA = 0.29056 - 0.08775*0.152 = 0.277222\n  0.29441 - Z_RA              = 0.017188\n  0.40768 * 0.017188          = 0.00700720384\n  R = 6.02214076e23 * 1.380649e-23 = 8.31446261815324 J/(mol*K)\n  R*Tc/Pc = 8.31446261815324 * 369.83 / 4.248e6 = 0.000723855393143035\n  c = 0.00700720384 * 0.000723855393143035 = 5.072202290436595e-06 m**3/mol\n\nThe shift is positive: this component's untranslated volume is too large, so `v_corr = v - c` shrinks it.\n", 'tolerance': 1e-12, 'inputs': {'omega': 0.152, 'Tc': 369.83, 'Pc': 4248000.0}, 'expected': {'c': 5.072202290436595e-06}},
        "implementations": {'python': 'azoth.eos.srk_peneloux_shift', 'rust': 'azoth_eos::srk_peneloux_shift'},
        "tests": [{'id': 'propane_worked_example', 'type': 'worked_example', 'status': 'active'}, {'id': 'methane_at_a_very_different_acentric_factor', 'type': 'reference', 'status': 'active', 'source': "Derived from the equation above, at methane's acentric factor, omega = 0.01142, Tc = 190.56 K, Pc = 4.599e6 Pa:\n\n  Z_RA = 0.29056 - 0.08775*0.01142 = 0.289557895\n  0.29441 - Z_RA = 0.004852105\n  0.40768 * 0.004852105 = 0.0019781061664\n  R*Tc/Pc = 8.31446261815324 * 190.56 / 4.599e6 = 0.00034454321694427266\n  c = 0.0019781061664 * 0.00034454321694427266 = 6.814784334802521e-07 m**3/mol\n", 'tolerance': 1e-12, 'inputs': {'omega': 0.01142, 'Tc': 190.56, 'Pc': 4599000.0}, 'expected': {'c': 6.814784334802521e-07}}, {'id': 'round_trip_units', 'type': 'property', 'property': 'unit_round_trip', 'status': 'active'}],
    },
    {
        "id": 'eos.srk_z_factor',
        "name": 'Soave-Redlich-Kwong compressibility factor',
        "equation": 'z**3 - z**2 + (a_reduced - b_reduced - b_reduced**2)*z - a_reduced*b_reduced = 0',
        "source": {'standard': 'Redlich, O.; Kwong, J. N. S. (1949)', 'edition': 'On the Thermodynamics of Solutions. V. Chemical Reviews, 44(1), 233-244', 'doi': '10.1021/cr60137a013'},
        "inputs": {'a_reduced': {'unit': 'dimensionless', 'description': "The cubic's attraction parameter `A`, from `eos.srk_alpha_ab`, dimensionless and already carrying the composition.\n"}, 'b_reduced': {'unit': 'dimensionless', 'description': "The cubic's repulsion parameter `B`, from `eos.srk_alpha_ab`.\n"}},
        "outputs": {'z_min': {'unit': 'dimensionless', 'description': 'The smallest *admissible* real root; roots at or below `b_reduced` are discarded, because `z = b_reduced` is the zero-volume limit.\n'}, 'z_max': {'unit': 'dimensionless', 'description': 'The largest admissible real root, named for its position in the ordered root set rather than as "liquid" or "vapour".\n'}, 'root_structure': {'type': 'enum', 'values': ['one_root', 'three_roots'], 'description': 'How many admissible real roots the cubic has: one, or three.'}, 'iterations': {'unit': 'dimensionless', 'description': 'solver iterations performed'}, 'converged': {'type': 'boolean', 'description': 'whether the iteration met the tolerance the solver block declares'}, 'residual': {'unit': 'dimensionless', 'description': 'the residual at the final iterate'}},
        "valid_range": [{'quantity': 'b_reduced', 'min': 0, 'min_inclusive': False, 'severity': 'error', 'rationale': '`B` is zero only at zero pressure, where the cubic degenerates to `z**3 - z**2 = z**2 (z - 1)` and the answer is trivial rather than wrong.\n'}, {'quantity': 'a_reduced', 'min': 0, 'min_inclusive': True, 'severity': 'error', 'rationale': '`A` is a squared term times a positive coefficient, so it cannot be negative for any real input to `eos.srk_alpha_ab`; zero is permitted as the hard-sphere limit.\n'}],
        "assumptions": ['the cubic has a real root above `b_reduced`. It is a theorem for `B > 0` rather than an assumption: the polynomial evaluates to `-2*B**2` at `z = b_reduced` and tends to positive infinity.\n', 'the caller has applied a mixing rule if the system is a mixture; this calc takes `A` and `B` already composition-weighted.\n', '**`z_min` and `z_max` are the extreme roots, not the stable phases.** Which root has the lower Gibbs energy is a question about the mixture and is not answered here.\n', "**near the critical point the answer is not well determined, and this calc cannot tell.** The cubic's constants place a triple root there.\n"],
        "references": ['Redlich, O.; Kwong, J. N. S. (1949). \\"On the Thermodynamics of Solutions. V.\\" Chemical Reviews 44(1), 233-244. DOI 10.1021/cr60137a013.', 'Soave, G. (1972). \\"Equilibrium constants from a modified Redlich-Kwong equation of state.\\" Chemical Engineering Science 27(6), 1197-1203. DOI 10.1016/0009-2509(72)80096-4.'],
        "solver": {'kind': 'cubic_roots', 'tolerance': 1e-12, 'max_iterations': 50, 'convergence': 'relative'},
        "data": None,
        "worked_example": {'source': 'derived from the equation above', 'derivation': 'The propane-like state from `eos.srk_alpha_ab`: A = 0.19315231739218255, B = 0.02707510936404929.\nThe cubic is then\n\n  z**3 - z**2 + (0.19315231739218255 - 0.02707510936404929 - 0.02707510936404929**2) z\n       - 0.19315231739218255 * 0.02707510936404929 = 0\n\nwhose coefficients evaluate to\n\n  z**3 - z**2 + 0.16534458490361234 z - 0.005229977236994362 = 0\n\nThis has three real roots; all exceed B, so all three are admissible and `root_structure` is `three_roots`:\n\n  z_min = 0.04171316701447374\n  z_max = 0.8019551972557889\n\nThe middle root is discarded: it is a real root of the polynomial, not a state this equation of state describes.\n', 'tolerance': 1e-12, 'inputs': {'a_reduced': 0.19315231739218255, 'b_reduced': 0.02707510936404929}, 'expected': {'z_min': 0.04171316701447374, 'z_max': 0.8019551972557889}},
        "implementations": {'python': 'azoth.eos.srk_z_factor', 'rust': 'azoth_eos::srk_z_factor'},
        "tests": [{'id': 'propane_like_three_root_worked_example', 'type': 'worked_example', 'status': 'active'}, {'id': 'a_single_root_above_the_critical_temperature', 'type': 'reference', 'status': 'active', 'source': 'Derived from the equation above, at A = 0.07492303219500959 and B = 0.02888011665498591, for methane at Tr = 1.5 and Pr = 0.5:\n\n  z**3 - z**2 + 0.04520883094020312 z - 0.002164057351498384 = 0\n\nhas one real root, z = 0.9550349515000371, and two complex ones.\n', 'tolerance': 1e-12, 'inputs': {'a_reduced': 0.07492303219500959, 'b_reduced': 0.02888011665498591}, 'expected': {'z_min': 0.9550349515000371, 'z_max': 0.9550349515000371}}, {'id': 'round_trip_units', 'type': 'property', 'property': 'unit_round_trip', 'status': 'skipped', 'skip_reason': 'Every input and every output is dimensionless, so there is no unit to convert. Declared rather than omitted so the omission is a recorded decision.\n'}],
    },
    {
        "id": 'eos.twu_kappa',
        "name": 'Twu attraction-parameter coefficient',
        "equation": 'kappa = 0.48 + 1.574*omega - 0.175*omega**2',
        "source": {'standard': 'Twu, C. H.; Bluck, D.; Cunningham, J. R.; Coon, J. E. (1991)', 'edition': 'A Cubic Equation of State with a New Alpha Function and a New Mixing Rule. Fluid Phase Equilibria, 69, 33-50', 'doi': '10.1016/0378-3812(91)90024-2'},
        "inputs": {'omega': {'unit': 'dimensionless', 'description': 'Acentric factor of the pure component, as defined by Pitzer at a reduced temperature of 0.7.\n'}},
        "outputs": {'kappa': {'unit': 'dimensionless', 'description': "Twu's coefficient, for the same Soave alpha form: alpha = (1 + kappa*(1 - sqrt(Tr)))**2.\n"}},
        "valid_range": [{'quantity': 'kappa', 'min': 0, 'min_inclusive': False, 'severity': 'warning', 'rationale': 'The polynomial passes through zero at omega = -0.296092 and omega = 9.290661, and kappa is negative below the lower one.\n'}],
        "assumptions": ['the acentric factor supplied is correct for the component intended. NOT CHECKED.\n', "the coefficient is Twu's, which differs from Soave's only in the last constant: 0.175 against 0.176.\n", 'the range of acentric factors over which the correlation was fitted is NOT established, and no bound asserts one.\n'],
        "references": ['Twu, C. H.; Bluck, D.; Cunningham, J. R.; Coon, J. E. (1991). \\"A Cubic Equation of State with a New Alpha Function and a New Mixing Rule.\\" Fluid Phase Equilibria 69, 33-50. DOI 10.1016/0378-3812(91)90024-2.', 'NeqSim 3.20.0, `neqsim.thermo.component.attractiveeosterm.AttractiveTermTwu`.'],
        "solver": None,
        "data": None,
        "worked_example": {'source': 'derived from the equation above', 'derivation': 'Propane-like, omega = 0.152:\n\n  1.574 * omega    = 1.574 * 0.152     = 0.239248\n  omega**2         = 0.152 * 0.152     = 0.023104\n  0.175 * omega**2 = 0.175 * 0.023104  = 0.0040432\n  0.48 + 0.239248  = 0.719248\n  0.719248 - 0.0040432 = 0.7152048\n\nkappa = 0.7152048.\n', 'tolerance': 1e-12, 'inputs': {'omega': 0.152}, 'expected': {'kappa': 0.7152048}},
        "implementations": {'python': 'azoth.eos.twu_kappa', 'rust': 'azoth_eos::twu_kappa'},
        "tests": [{'id': 'propane_worked_example', 'type': 'worked_example', 'status': 'active'}, {'id': 'methane_at_a_very_different_acentric_factor', 'type': 'reference', 'status': 'active', 'source': "Derived from the equation above, at methane's acentric factor, omega = 0.01142:\n\n  kappa = 0.48 + 1.574*0.01142 - 0.175*0.01142**2 = 0.49795225713\n", 'tolerance': 1e-12, 'inputs': {'omega': 0.01142}, 'expected': {'kappa': 0.49795225712999996}}, {'id': 'round_trip_units', 'type': 'property', 'property': 'unit_round_trip', 'status': 'skipped', 'skip_reason': 'Both the input and the output are dimensionless, so there is no unit to convert. Declared rather than omitted so the omission is a recorded decision.\n'}],
    },
    {
        "id": 'eos.vdw1f_mix_binary',
        "name": 'van der Waals one-fluid mixing, for a binary',
        "equation": 'a_mix = z1**2*a1 + 2*z1*(1 - z1)*(1 - k12)*(a1*a2)**0.5 + (1 - z1)**2*a2\nb_mix = z1*b1 + (1 - z1)*b2\n',
        "source": {'standard': 'van der Waals, J. D. (1890); and the conventional one-fluid treatment in any modern cubic-EOS text', 'edition': "The classical mixing rule. Its modern form, and the role of k12, are set out in e.g. Poling, Prausnitz & O'Connell, The Properties of Gases and Liquids, 5th ed., ch. 5."},
        "inputs": {'z1': {'unit': 'dimensionless', 'description': 'Mole fraction of component 1 in the phase being mixed; component 2 is `1 - z1`, so there is no `z2` input.\n'}, 'a1': {'unit': 'dimensionless', 'description': "the cubic's attraction parameter `A` for component 1, from `eos.pr_alpha_ab`"}, 'a2': {'unit': 'dimensionless', 'description': 'the same for component 2'}, 'b1': {'unit': 'dimensionless', 'description': "the cubic's repulsion parameter `B` for component 1"}, 'b2': {'unit': 'dimensionless', 'description': 'the same for component 2'}, 'k12': {'unit': 'dimensionless', 'description': 'The binary interaction parameter for the pair, supplied by the caller; conventionally small and positive for light-hydrocarbon pairs.\n'}},
        "outputs": {'a_mix': {'unit': 'dimensionless', 'description': "the mixture's attraction parameter, `1/2 * sum_i sum_j z_i z_j a_ij`"}, 'b_mix': {'unit': 'dimensionless', 'description': "the mixture's repulsion parameter, the mole-fraction-weighted mean of the pure ones - linear and parameter-free, with no `l12` covolume correction.\n"}},
        "valid_range": [{'quantity': 'z1', 'min': 0, 'max': 1, 'severity': 'error', 'rationale': 'A mole fraction. Both ends are admitted, since a pure component is a legitimate limiting case and `z1 = 0` or `1` must reproduce the pure-component parameters.\n'}, {'quantity': 'a_mix', 'min': 0, 'min_inclusive': False, 'severity': 'error', 'rationale': '`a_mix` is a quadratic form in `(z1, z2)` with matrix `[[a1, a12], [a12, a2]]` where `a12 = (1 - k12)*sqrt(a1*a2)`, so it is non-negative for every composition exactly when `0 <= k12 <= 2`.\n'}],
        "assumptions": ['`k12` is a fitted parameter for the pair intended, and its correctness is NOT CHECKED - this library ships no values for it and has no second source.', 'The convention is `a12 = (1 - k12)*sqrt(a1*a2)`; authors differ, so a value copied from a table without checking its convention is a plausible-looking wrong number.\n', "the two components' `A` and `B` must come from the *same* equation of state and the same temperature.\n", 'this is not a pressure or temperature dependence. `A` and `B` carry those; this calc only combines them.', 'no volume translation is applied, and no `l12` covolume correction.'],
        "references": ['van der Waals, J. D. (1890). "On the Continuity of the Gaseous and Liquid States." (the origin of the quadratic mixing form; the citation is unconfirmed - see `notes`)', 'Poling, B. E.; Prausnitz, J. M.; O\'Connell, J. P. \\"The Properties of Gases and Liquids\\", 5th ed.'],
        "solver": None,
        "data": None,
        "worked_example": {'source': 'derived from the equation above', 'derivation': 'The propane-like and methane-like reduced parameters already in this namespace, at equal-ish proportions, with a k12 chosen to be round.\n**The value 0.05 is illustrative and is not a fitted parameter for this - or any - pair.**\n\n  z1 = 0.6,  a1 = 0.20206500174625697,  b1 = 0.02431127309496514\n            a2 = 0.08448417260831159,  b2 = 0.025932024634629486\n  k12 = 0.05\n\nThe three terms of the double sum, in order:\n\n  z1**2 * a1                     = 0.36 * 0.20206500174625697 = 0.0727434006286525\n  sqrt(a1*a2)                    = sqrt(0.20206500174625697 * 0.08448417260831159)\n                                 = 0.1306571639276988\n  2*z1*(1 - z1)*(1 - k12)*sqrt   = 2 * 0.6 * 0.4 * 0.95 * 0.1306571639276988\n                                 = 0.05957966675103065\n  (1 - z1)**2 * a2               = 0.16 * 0.08448417260831159 = 0.013517467617329857\n\na_mix = 0.0727434006286525 + 0.05957966675103065 + 0.013517467617329857\n      = 0.14584053499701302\n\nb_mix = 0.6 * 0.02431127309496514 + 0.4 * 0.025932024634629486\n      = 0.014586763856979084 + 0.010372809853851795\n      = 0.02495957371083088\n', 'tolerance': 1e-12, 'inputs': {'z1': 0.6, 'a1': 0.20206500174625697, 'a2': 0.08448417260831159, 'b1': 0.02431127309496514, 'b2': 0.025932024634629486, 'k12': 0.05}, 'expected': {'a_mix': 0.14584053499701302, 'b_mix': 0.02495957371083088}},
        "implementations": {'python': 'azoth.eos.vdw1f_mix_binary', 'rust': 'azoth_eos::vdw1f_mix_binary'},
        "tests": [{'id': 'propane_like_and_methane_like_with_k12', 'type': 'worked_example', 'status': 'active'}, {'id': 'a_pure_component_reproduces_its_own_parameters', 'type': 'reference', 'status': 'active', 'source': "Derived from the equations above: at `z1 = 1` the quadratic form must collapse to component 1's own parameters and at `z1 = 0` to component 2's.\n\n  z1 = 1: a_mix = 1**2*a1 = a1 = 0.20206500174625697,  b_mix = b1 = 0.02431127309496514\n  z1 = 0: a_mix = a2 = 0.08448417260831159,            b_mix = b2 = 0.025932024634629486\n", 'tolerance': 1e-15, 'inputs': {'z1': 1.0, 'a1': 0.20206500174625697, 'a2': 0.08448417260831159, 'b1': 0.02431127309496514, 'b2': 0.025932024634629486, 'k12': 0.05}, 'expected': {'a_mix': 0.20206500174625697, 'b_mix': 0.02431127309496514}}, {'id': 'ethane_and_a_large_k12', 'type': 'reference', 'status': 'active', 'source': 'Derived from the equations above, at a different composition, different parameters and a much larger interaction parameter:\n\n  z1 = 0.25, a1 = 0.115, a2 = 0.31, b1 = 0.018, b2 = 0.041, k12 = 0.4\n\n  z1**2*a1                   = 0.0625 * 0.115 = 0.0071875\n  sqrt(a1*a2)                = sqrt(0.115 * 0.31) = 0.18881207588499208\n  2*z1*(1-z1)*(1-k12)*sqrt   = 2 * 0.25 * 0.75 * 0.6 * 0.18881207588499208\n                             = 0.04248271707412321\n  (1-z1)**2*a2               = 0.5625 * 0.31 = 0.174375\n  a_mix                      = 0.0071875 + 0.04248271707412321 + 0.174375\n                             = 0.22404521707412323\n  b_mix                      = 0.25*0.018 + 0.75*0.041 = 0.0045 + 0.03075 = 0.03525\n`a_mix = 0.22404521707412323`.\n', 'tolerance': 1e-12, 'inputs': {'z1': 0.25, 'a1': 0.115, 'a2': 0.31, 'b1': 0.018, 'b2': 0.041, 'k12': 0.4}, 'expected': {'a_mix': 0.22404521707412323, 'b_mix': 0.03525}}, {'id': 'monotonic', 'type': 'property', 'property': 'monotonic', 'status': 'active'}, {'id': 'round_trip_units', 'type': 'property', 'property': 'unit_round_trip', 'status': 'skipped', 'skip_reason': 'Every input and every output is dimensionless, so there is no unit to convert. Declared rather than omitted so the omission is a recorded decision.\n'}],
    },
    {
        "id": 'hydraulics.choked_flow_area',
        "name": 'Choked-flow throat area for an ideal gas',
        "equation": 'A = m_dot / (sqrt(k * rho0 * P0) * (2 / (k + 1))**((k + 1) / (2 * (k - 1))))',
        "source": {'standard': 'the isentropic critical-flow relation from gas dynamics; API 520 / ISO 4126 for relief valve sizing, which this calc does NOT implement'},
        "inputs": {'m_dot': {'unit': 'kg/s', 'description': 'Mass flow rate the throat must pass.'}, 'P0': {'unit': 'Pa', 'description': 'Stagnation (upstream total) pressure.'}, 'rho0': {'unit': 'kg/m**3', 'description': 'Stagnation density.'}, 'k': {'unit': 'dimensionless', 'description': 'Isentropic exponent, the ratio of specific heats cp/cv.'}},
        "outputs": {'a': {'unit': 'm**2', 'description': 'Throat area required to pass m_dot when the flow is choked.'}},
        "valid_range": [{'quantity': 'm_dot', 'min': 0, 'severity': 'error', 'rationale': 'flow is proportional to area, so a negative rate would give a negative area. Zero is allowed and correct: passing no mass needs no throat.\n'}, {'quantity': 'P0', 'min': 0, 'min_inclusive': False, 'severity': 'error', 'rationale': 'stagnation pressure enters under the square root, so zero gives no mass flux and would make the required area infinite; negative would make the root imaginary.\n'}, {'quantity': 'rho0', 'min': 0, 'min_inclusive': False, 'severity': 'error', 'rationale': 'stagnation density likewise enters under the square root and must be positive'}, {'quantity': 'k', 'min': 1, 'min_inclusive': False, 'severity': 'error', 'rationale': '`k - 1` appears in a denominator in the exponent, so `k = 1` is a division by zero.'}, {'quantity': 'k', 'max': 1.6666666666666667, 'severity': 'warning', 'code': 'OUT_OF_VALID_RANGE', 'rationale': 'No known gas has an isentropic exponent above 5/3, the monatomic limit.'}],
        "assumptions": ['The gas is ideal, with constant specific heats.', 'The flow is choked: the pressure downstream of the throat is below the critical ratio.', 'The expansion is isentropic: adiabatic and frictionless through the throat.', 'the area is the throat - the minimum area - and the flow there is one-dimensional and normal to it', 'There are no inlet or outlet losses, no back-pressure correction and no rupture-disc or combination factors.', 'The gas is single-phase at the throat.', '`k` is constant through the expansion.', 'Stagnation conditions upstream: the approach velocity is negligible.'],
        "references": ['No specific source is claimed: the isentropic critical-flow relation is standard compressible-flow theory.', 'API 520 Part I, Sizing and Selection of Pressure-Relieving Devices, and ISO 4126, Safety devices for protection against excessive pressure.'],
        "solver": None,
        "data": None,
        "worked_example": {'source': 'derived from the equation above', 'derivation': 'Air-like conditions at a convenient size: 1 kg/s of gas with an isentropic exponent of 1.4, at 1 MPa of stagnation pressure and 10 kg/m**3 of stagnation density.\n\n  (k + 1) / (2 * (k - 1)) = 2.4 / 0.8 = 3            exactly\n  2 / (k + 1)             = 2 / 2.4 = 5/6            exactly\n  (5/6)**3                = 125/216 = 0.5787037037037037\n\n  k * rho0 * P0 = 1.4 * 10.0 * 1000000.0 = 14000000.0\n  sqrt(14000000.0) = 3741.657386773941\n\n  G* = 3741.657386773941 * 0.5787037037037037 = 2165.310987716401 kg/(m**2 s)\n\n  A = 1.0 / 2165.310987716401 = 0.00046182742602466937 m**2', 'tolerance': 1e-12, 'inputs': {'m_dot': 1.0, 'P0': 1000000.0, 'rho0': 10.0, 'k': 1.4}, 'expected': {'a': 0.00046182742602466937}},
        "implementations": {'python': 'azoth.hydraulics.choked_flow_area', 'rust': 'azoth_hydraulics::choked_flow_area'},
        "tests": [{'id': 'air_like_conditions_worked_example', 'type': 'worked_example', 'status': 'active'}, {'id': 'different_in_every_input', 'type': 'reference', 'status': 'active', 'source': 'Derived from the equation above at an isentropic exponent of 1.3, 2.5 kg/s, half a megapascal and 6 kg/m**3.\n\n  (k + 1) / (2 * (k - 1)) = 2.3 / 0.6 = 3.8333333333333326\n  2 / (k + 1)             = 2 / 2.3 = 0.8695652173913044\n  that to that power      = 0.5852277961374616\n  sqrt(1.3 * 6.0 * 500000.0) = sqrt(3900000.0) = 1974.84176581315\n  G* = 1974.84176581315 * 0.5852277961374616 = 1155.7322943270428 kg/(m**2 s)\n  A  = 2.5 / 1155.7322943270428 = 0.0021631306940814474 m**2', 'tolerance': 1e-12, 'inputs': {'m_dot': 2.5, 'P0': 500000.0, 'rho0': 6.0, 'k': 1.3}, 'expected': {'a': 0.0021631306940814474}}, {'id': 'agrees_with_the_stagnation_temperature_form', 'type': 'reference', 'status': 'active', 'source': 'Derived from the stagnation-temperature form of the relation, for air at 1 MPa and 300 K with R = 287 J/(kg K).\n\n  G* = P0 * sqrt(k / (R T0)) * (2/(k+1))**((k+1)/(2(k-1)))\n\n  rho0 = 1000000.0 / (287.0 * 300.0) = 11.614401858304298 kg/m**3\n  textbook form: G* = 2333.558560606226 kg/(m**2 s)\n  A = 1.0 / 2333.558560606226 = 0.00042853006428954324 m**2', 'tolerance': 1e-12, 'inputs': {'m_dot': 1.0, 'P0': 1000000.0, 'rho0': 11.614401858304298, 'k': 1.4}, 'expected': {'a': 0.00042853006428954324}}, {'id': 'monotonic', 'type': 'property', 'property': 'monotonic', 'status': 'active'}, {'id': 'round_trip_units', 'type': 'property', 'property': 'unit_round_trip', 'status': 'active'}],
    },
    {
        "id": 'hydraulics.control_valve_cv',
        "name": 'Liquid flow through a control valve from its flow coefficient',
        "equation": 'q = CV_TO_SI * Cv * sqrt(dP / SG)',
        "source": {'standard': 'the liquid sizing equation for control valves; IEC 60534 for the coefficient tables, which this calc does not implement'},
        "inputs": {'Cv': {'unit': 'dimensionless', 'description': 'Valve flow coefficient in the US convention: the flow of water in US gallons per minute at a pressure drop of one pound per square inch.'}, 'dP': {'unit': 'Pa', 'description': 'Pressure drop across the valve.'}, 'SG': {'unit': 'dimensionless', 'description': 'Specific gravity of the liquid: its density divided by the density of water at 15.6 C (60 F), the reference the coefficient convention uses.'}},
        "outputs": {'q': {'unit': 'm**3/s', 'description': 'volumetric flow rate through the valve'}},
        "valid_range": [{'quantity': 'Cv', 'min': 0, 'min_inclusive': False, 'severity': 'error', 'rationale': 'Flow is proportional to the coefficient, so a non-positive one gives no flow or negative flow.'}, {'quantity': 'dP', 'min': 0, 'severity': 'error', 'rationale': 'a negative drop would mean flow in the other direction, which is a different calculation and not the one named here. Zero is allowed and correct: no pressure difference drives no flow.\n'}, {'quantity': 'SG', 'min': 0, 'min_inclusive': False, 'severity': 'error', 'rationale': 'specific gravity is a divisor inside the square root, so zero is singular and negative would make the root imaginary.\n'}],
        "assumptions": ['single-phase liquid flow. A flashing or cavitating liquid is not described by this relation at all, and neither is a two-phase inlet.', 'The flow is non-choked: above a limiting pressure drop the flow through a valve stops rising with the pressure difference.', 'fully turbulent flow through the valve, which is what a published coefficient assumes. At low Reynolds numbers the coefficient falls off and the true flow is smaller.', 'The coefficient is for the valve at the travel and trim it is actually installed at.', 'The installed valve has no attached fittings whose loss is being counted twice.', '`SG` is relative to water at 15.6 C, the reference the `Cv` convention uses. A caller working from density ratios at another reference temperature has a small systematic offset, not a random error.', 'the pressure drop is the difference across the valve itself, between the tappings the coefficient is defined against, and not the difference across a sub-circuit containing it'],
        "references": ['IEC 60534, Industrial-process control valves, Part 2-1: Flow capacity.', 'No specific source is claimed: Q = Cv * sqrt(dP/SG) is standard control-valve sizing.', 'Derived, not cited: 1 US gallon = 3.785411784 L and 1 lbf = 4.4482216152605 N are both exact definitions.'],
        "solver": None,
        "data": None,
        "worked_example": {'source': 'derived from the equation above', 'derivation': 'Water at Cv = 10, SG = 1 and a pressure drop of exactly one pound per square inch.\nThe constant first:\n\n  1 US gallon      = 3.785411784e-3 m**3            (definition)\n  1 gpm            = 3.785411784e-3 / 60 = 6.30901964e-5 m**3/s\n  1 lbf            = 4.4482216152605 N              (definition)\n  1 in             = 0.0254 m                       (definition)\n  1 psi            = 4.4482216152605 / 0.0254**2 = 6894.757293168361 Pa\n  sqrt(1 psi)      = 83.03467524575719\n  CV_TO_SI         = 6.30901964e-05 / 83.03467524575719 = 7.59805421208337e-07\n\nThen the example, at Cv = 10, dP = 1 psi and SG = 1:\n\n  q = 7.59805421208337e-7 * 10 * sqrt(6894.757293168361 / 1)\n    = 7.59805421208337e-07 * 10 * 83.03467524575719\n    = 6.309019640000001e-4 m**3/s\n\nThat is 10 gpm.', 'tolerance': 1e-12, 'inputs': {'Cv': 10.0, 'dP': 6894.757293168361, 'SG': 1.0}, 'expected': {'q': 0.0006309019640000001}},
        "implementations": {'python': 'azoth.hydraulics.control_valve_cv', 'rust': 'azoth_hydraulics::control_valve_cv'},
        "tests": [{'id': 'unit_drop_at_unit_specific_gravity_worked_example', 'type': 'worked_example', 'status': 'active'}, {'id': 'realistic_bar_of_drop', 'type': 'reference', 'status': 'active', 'source': 'Derived from the equation above at Cv = 10, SG = 1 and 1 bar of drop (100000 Pa).\nThrough the SI constant:\n  q = 7.59805421208337e-7 * 10 * sqrt(100000) = 0.00240271570956195 m**3/s\n\nThrough the convention, converting the drop first:\n  100000 Pa / 6894.757293168361 Pa per psi = 14.503773773020923 psi\n  Q = 10 * sqrt(14.503773773020923) = 38.0838204136887 gpm\n  q = 38.0838204136887 * 6.30901964e-05 = 0.0024027157095619493 m**3/s', 'tolerance': 1e-12, 'inputs': {'Cv': 10.0, 'dP': 100000.0, 'SG': 1.0}, 'expected': {'q': 0.00240271570956195}}, {'id': 'a_coefficient_of_one_passes_one_gallon_per_minute', 'type': 'reference', 'status': 'active', 'source': 'Derived from the definition of Cv: a coefficient of 1 passes 1 gpm of water at 1 psi of drop, which in SI is 6.30901964e-5 m**3/s.', 'tolerance': 1e-12, 'inputs': {'Cv': 1.0, 'dP': 6894.757293168361, 'SG': 1.0}, 'expected': {'q': 6.30901964e-05}}, {'id': 'denser_liquid_passes_less', 'type': 'reference', 'status': 'active', 'source': 'Derived from the equation above at a specific gravity of 1.2.\n  sqrt(6894.757293168361 / 1.2) = 75.79994114536171\n  q(SG = 1.2) = 7.59805421208337e-07 * 10 * 75.79994114536171\n              = 0.0005759320620951871 m**3/s', 'tolerance': 1e-12, 'inputs': {'Cv': 10.0, 'dP': 6894.757293168361, 'SG': 1.2}, 'expected': {'q': 0.0005759320620951871}}, {'id': 'monotonic', 'type': 'property', 'property': 'monotonic', 'status': 'active'}, {'id': 'round_trip_units', 'type': 'property', 'property': 'unit_round_trip', 'status': 'active'}],
    },
